  "notes": "The pre-Cheiron UK chart template: 80–130 straight, backbeat on 2&4, with six documented tonics across the uptempo majors and the moodier minors, so `keys` is authored. The drum sentence is \"programmed hip-hop-lite kit — kick on 1 and the '&' of 2, snare 2&4 with a heavy clap layer, 16th hat with an open hat on 4&; occasional scratch or vinyl-noise as percussion\", and all four claims are encoded: the second anchor on \"2&\", a widened clap offset, a pinned 16th base with the open hat on \"4&\" only, and a perc lane for the scratch texture. The bass is the other identity — \"sampled/played funk bass, syncopated 16ths in the verses, root-heavy under the chorus; slides into the downbeat of each 4-bar cell\" — which is `independent_riff` with a real glide probability and a high anticipation. Call-and-response is called \"the core device\", so the melody is a `call_response` structure with a high density and traded two-bar phrases. ⚠ `confidence` is medium because the research rates it high-medium and marks the tempo **[LOW-CONF]**: several catalogue readings are doubled (144 and 169) and the entry folds them to 72 and 85, which is why the session floor sits at 80 rather than in the 140s. ⚠ The **INFERRED** parameters are all drum probabilities and `bpm.mode` 110.",
